osmbase
Copyright 2026 Jesper Wisborg Krogh

This product includes software developed by third parties, and renders data
published by third parties.  The two are listed separately and deliberately:
a software licence obliges this PROGRAM, and a data licence obliges its
OUTPUT.  Collapsing them is how the obligation attached to a rendered image
gets forgotten by whoever ships the image.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Software
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The LIBRARY requires two modules, both from the Go project.  The COMMAND
requires three more, for its progress bars.  All five are written out in full
here so that another arriving is a visible edit to this file rather than a
quiet line in go.sum, and the dependency target in the Makefile admits them
BY NAME for the same reason -- and checks that the command's three are
compiled into the command alone, never into a library package, so a program
built on this library links none of them.

Library and command:

golang.org/x/image         Copyright (c) 2009 The Go Authors.  BSD 3-Clause.
                           The `vector` package rasterizes every filled path
                           this library draws.  Keeping the library to this
                           and x/text is a deliberate constraint -- see
                           docs/architecture.md, "Almost zero third-party
                           modules".

golang.org/x/text          Copyright (c) 2009 The Go Authors.  BSD 3-Clause.

  Not imported directly.  It arrives under x/image/font/opentype, which needs
  x/image/font/sfnt, which needs x/text/encoding/charmap to read a font's
  character map.  The command parses a font because it draws place names, and
  the bitmap face it used before had no glyph for most of them: a map of
  Denmark came out reading "S0nder Lindskov".  A map that misspells the places
  on it is worse than a map with none, because the reader cannot tell which
  names are wrong.

  The LIBRARY still parses no font and draws no text without one being handed
  in, so a consumer that supplies its own face links neither the parser nor
  the font data.  This dependency is the command's.

Command only (cmd/osmbase):

github.com/wisborg/output  Copyright 2026 Jesper Wisborg Krogh.  Apache
                           License 2.0, the same licence as this project.
                           Its progress package draws the fetch progress
                           bars: redrawn in place on a terminal and never
                           wider than it, plain periodic lines when the
                           output is redirected.  fitdash and course draw
                           theirs with the same package.

github.com/mattn/go-runewidth
                           Copyright (c) 2016 Yasuhiro Matsumoto.  MIT.
                           Reached through output/progress, which measures a
                           line in terminal columns so it never wraps.

github.com/clipperhouse/uax29
                           Copyright (c) 2020 Matt Sherman.  MIT.  Reached
                           through output/progress and go-runewidth, which
                           split text into the characters a reader sees.

There are no other software dependencies.  The PMTiles reader, the Mapbox
Vector Tile decoder, the OpenStreetMap PBF reader and the rasterizer's path
and stroke construction are all written here, for reasons recorded in
docs/architecture.md.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Data
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The obligations below attach to anything rendered from data this library
reads -- an image, a video, a printed map -- and they travel with that output
to whoever receives it.  They are NOT discharged by this file alone.

OpenStreetMap              Map data is © OpenStreetMap contributors, available
                           under the Open Database License (ODbL).
                           https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright

                           A rendered image is a Produced Work under the ODbL,
                           so it must carry the credit above; share-alike does
                           not reach it.  The credit string travels in the
                           slice manifest rather than being written here as a
                           constant, so a slice built from another source
                           carries that source's own credit instead.

                           A DERIVED BOUNDARY FILE IS DIFFERENT.  The file
                           that "boundary/osm" builds from an OpenStreetMap
                           extract -- administrative outlines, written in this
                           module's own format -- is a Derivative Database,
                           not a Produced Work, because it IS the map data in
                           another shape rather than a picture made from it.
                           Share-alike attaches to it: whoever receives that
                           file must receive it under the ODbL.

                           Answering "which suburb is this coordinate in" FROM
                           that file is a Produced Work again, and needs the
                           credit only.  The distinction is the difference
                           between handing somebody the boundaries and telling
                           them a place name.

                           This is an obligation on the DATA and not on this
                           Apache-2.0 code.  It is also the first such
                           obligation in this project, and the command that
                           builds the file has to say so where the person
                           running it will see it.

Protomaps                  The public planet builds this library takes tiles
                           from are themselves a Produced Work over OpenStreet-
                           Map data.  https://protomaps.com

Natural Earth              Public domain.  Used two ways: by the Protomaps
                           builds for the lowest zoom levels, and DIRECTLY by
                           "osmbase boundaries", which downloads the country and
                           state outlines that let "osmbase locate" say a
                           coordinate is IN a country rather than near one.
                           No attribution is required -- "No permission is
                           needed to use Natural Earth.  Crediting the authors
                           is unnecessary" -- and nothing about using it
                           attaches to what a consumer does with the answers.
                           It is written here anyway, because this file is
                           where a reader looks to find out what went into the
                           output, and an entry that named only the indirect
                           use would have understated it.
                           https://www.naturalearthdata.com

Elevation                  Hillshading ("osmbase render --terrain") is drawn
                           from elevation that "osmbase fetch --terrain" copies
                           from Mapterhorn (https://mapterhorn.com), which
                           assembles some hundred and fifty open elevation
                           models into tiles.  Each has its own licence -- CC BY
                           4.0, public domain, open government licences, and
                           Copernicus's -- and none is share-alike or
                           non-commercial.  A shaded image owes Mapterhorn and
                           each source under the view; which ones is worked out
                           from Mapterhorn's coverage data and its source list,
                           attribution.json.  The image carries a short credit
                           pointing to Mapterhorn's list, as Mapterhorn's own
                           map does; "osmbase render" prints the full notice
                           for the view, which whoever publishes the image must
                           give with it.  The full list, with every source's
                           licence, is
                           https://download.mapterhorn.com/attribution.json

Copernicus GLO-30          The worldwide fallback under every other source.
                           Its licence asks for this sentence to be given with
                           anything produced from the data, and the full notice
                           "osmbase render" prints carries it wherever the data
                           is under the view:

                             produced using Copernicus WorldDEM-30 © DLR e.V.
